# `M154b` / `C2`, rewritten by `M159f` — the known-answer plant for `accept dialog` and
# `dismiss dialog`, and for the contract tflw's `D797` sharpened: *"arm a handler accepting the next
# native dialog; armings queue, one dialog each, in order; without one the browser auto-dismisses
# silently"*.
#
# **This file is meant to pass.** It is a plant because each assertion distinguishes a real tflw
# regression from correct behaviour, which the two dialog uses already in this repository do not:
#
#   `tests/mixed/storefront.tflw` and `tests/.env-specific/webv2-admin.tflw` each pair a
#   `dismiss dialog` with an `accept dialog` on a single confirm. The `accept` half is real — the
#   default is dismissal, so the delete cannot happen unless the step armed something. The `dismiss`
#   half proves nothing at all: the browser dismisses an unhandled dialog anyway, so that assertion
#   holds identically whether or not `dismiss dialog` did anything. Filed as `M154b-01`, and
#   **closed by the ORDER block below** (`D806e`) — the step is unobservable in isolation, and it is
#   observable in a queue, because which dialog it answers depends on where it was written.
#
# The target is `webV2/admin`'s bulk out-of-stock delete, whose form runs two `confirm()`s in one
# short-circuited handler and records which one it stopped at. Four states of the same button, and
# the set is what carries the claim — any one of them alone would be vacuous:
#
#   nothing armed          #1 dismissed by default, #2 never shown    -> `cancelled`
#   `accept` once          #1 accepted, #2 dismissed by default       -> `cancelled-final`
#   `dismiss` then `accept` #1 dismissed, #2 never shown              -> `cancelled` + a leftover arming
#   `accept` twice         both accepted                              -> the form submits, both products gone
#
# **The fourth was unreachable until `D797`** and is `M154b-02`: `armedDialog` was a single slot, so
# two consecutive `accept dialog` steps armed one handler, and the two dialogs come from a single
# `click` with no step boundary between them to arm again. Nothing refused the program — tflw
# accepted a script and silently did half of it. It is asserted here now, in a test of its own
# (`D806f`), because it is terminal: it deletes the products that make the button exist.

@constructs @dialog @mixed
test "one `accept dialog` accepts exactly one dialog; with none armed the first is dismissed"
  api POST /auth/login body { email: env(ADMIN_EMAIL), password: env(ADMIN_PW) }
  expect status equals 200
  capture body.accessToken as adminToken
  api GET /categories
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {adminToken}"
  expect status equals 200
  capture body[0].id as categoryId

  # Two out-of-stock products under one prefix that is unique **across runs**, so the list filter
  # below selects exactly these two and the bulk action can never reach the seed catalogue — the
  # route is scoped to `stock === 0` *and* to the filter, and this is the half of that fence the
  # test owns.
  #
  # `unique uuid` and not `unique("C2 Dialog Plant")`. The prefix form promises collision-safety
  # "across tests/workers/retries" and that list does not include *runs*: it is a run-scoped
  # counter, so two runs both produce `-0`. Against a database that survives between runs — which
  # is every run of this plant after the first — the second run then created a second `… alpha` and
  # the filter matched two elements. tflw's contract says exactly what it covers and this plant had
  # assumed one word more. `unique uuid` guarantees distinctness instead.
  let prefix = unique uuid
  api POST /products body { name: "{prefix} alpha", price: 5.00, stock: 0, categoryId: {categoryId} }
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {adminToken}"
  expect status equals 201
  capture body.id as alphaId
  api POST /products body { name: "{prefix} beta", price: 6.00, stock: 0, categoryId: {categoryId} }
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {adminToken}"
  expect status equals 201
  capture body.id as betaId

  open "/login"
  fill form
    | "Email"    | env(ADMIN_EMAIL) |
    | "Password" | env(ADMIN_PW)    |
  click button "Log in"
  expect button "Log out" is visible

  open "/products?q={prefix}"
  expect text "{prefix} alpha" is visible
  expect text "{prefix} beta" is visible
  expect css "#bulk-delete-state[data-state='idle']" is visible

  # THE CONTROL. Nothing armed, so Playwright dismisses the first confirm and the second is never
  # shown. On its own this measures Playwright, not tflw — it earns its place as the other half of
  # the contrast below, and the two assertions are only meaningful as a pair.
  click button "Delete 2 out-of-stock"
  expect css "#bulk-delete-state[data-state='cancelled']" is visible

  # THE PLANT. One arming, same button. The first dialog is accepted by the armed handler and the
  # second falls through to the default dismissal, which is exactly what `cancelled-final` records —
  # a state the control cannot reach and a sticky handler would skip past.
  accept dialog
  click button "Delete 2 out-of-stock"
  expect css "#bulk-delete-state[data-state='cancelled-final']" is visible

  # `M159f` / `C43` — THE ORDER, and what replaced the overwrite this block used to grade.
  #
  # Until `D797` an arming was a single **slot**, so `dismiss dialog` did one thing its absence
  # could not: overwrite a prior arming. That is what this block graded, and the queue removed the
  # mechanism — measured red on 2026-08-30, at this block alone, because the accept is no longer
  # discarded (dialog 1 accepted, dialog 2 dismissed by the second arming, `cancelled-final`).
  #
  # The replacement grades the property the queue actually promises — *each arming is consumed by
  # exactly one dialog, IN ORDER* — by putting the dismissal FIRST:
  #
  #   real            #1 dismissed -> the form returns false, #2 is never raised   -> `cancelled`
  #   a no-op dismiss #1 takes the ACCEPT, #2 falls through to the default         -> `cancelled-final`
  #   a stack         #1 takes the accept as well                                  -> `cancelled-final`
  #
  # `cancelled-final` is the state the block immediately above just produced, so the wrong answer is
  # reachable in this very run rather than hypothetical. One block, three wrong implementations —
  # strictly more than the overwrite, which could only tell a real `dismiss` from a no-op one.
  #
  # **Dismissal is still unobservable in isolation, and `M154b-01`'s finding stands.** The browser's
  # unhandled default IS dismissal, so `accept, dismiss` and a lone `accept` both land on
  # `cancelled-final`. What closes the row is that ORDER makes the step's effect visible without
  # needing a state the default cannot reach.
  dismiss dialog
  accept dialog
  click button "Delete 2 out-of-stock"
  expect css "#bulk-delete-state[data-state='cancelled']" is visible

  # `M159b` / `D798` — and this is what makes the line above say something the state alone cannot.
  # `dialog message` reads the last dialog of this attempt: with the dismissal consumed by confirm
  # #1 the run never sees confirm #2, so the last message is the FIRST confirm's. Under any of the
  # three wrong implementations above, confirm #2 was raised and this reads its text instead.
  expect dialog message equals "Delete 2 out-of-stock product(s) matching this filter?"
  expect dialog type equals "confirm"

  # Neither state deleted anything, and this line would hold under both — which is why it is here as
  # a fence on the bulk route's blast radius and not as the claim about `accept dialog`.
  api GET /products/{alphaId}
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {adminToken}"
  expect status equals 200
  api GET /products/{betaId}
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {adminToken}"
  expect status equals 200

  # `M159f` / `D806g` — THE ACCEPT ABOVE IS NEVER CONSUMED, ON PURPOSE.
  #
  # The dismissal answered confirm #1 and the form stopped there, so the `accept dialog` written
  # after it is still queued when this test ends. tflw reports that as `warning[TF079]` against its
  # own line, and this plant is the only place in this repository that produces one — which is the
  # only kind of proof a code no `tflw check` can emit is able to have here (tflw `D802`, `D806d`).
  # The acceptance grader asserts the warning by code AND by line; nothing in the .tflw file can,
  # because a warning is not a step and does not change a verdict.
  #
  # Deliberately not "fixed" by arming in the other order: the leftover is the evidence.

@constructs @dialog @mixed
test "two armings answer two dialogs raised by one click, and the delete really happens"
  # `M154b-02`, closed. Both `confirm()`s come from a single `click`, so no step can sit between
  # them and two consecutive armings is the only spelling this language has. Under the single slot
  # the second arming overwrote the first, the second dialog fell through to the default dismissal,
  # and nothing anywhere refused the program — tflw accepted a script and silently did not do what
  # it said.
  #
  # A SECOND TEST rather than a fourth block of the one above, for two reasons that are both about
  # state rather than taste. This case is terminal — the products are deleted and the button they
  # made appear goes with them — and the block above deliberately leaves an arming unconsumed, which
  # under `D803` a later dialog in the same attempt would inherit.
  api POST /auth/login body { email: env(ADMIN_EMAIL), password: env(ADMIN_PW) }
  expect status equals 200
  capture body.accessToken as adminToken
  api GET /categories
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {adminToken}"
  expect status equals 200
  capture body[0].id as categoryId

  let prefix = unique uuid
  api POST /products body { name: "{prefix} gamma", price: 5.00, stock: 0, categoryId: {categoryId} }
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {adminToken}"
  expect status equals 201
  capture body.id as gammaId
  api POST /products body { name: "{prefix} delta", price: 6.00, stock: 0, categoryId: {categoryId} }
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {adminToken}"
  expect status equals 201
  capture body.id as deltaId

  open "/login"
  fill form
    | "Email"    | env(ADMIN_EMAIL) |
    | "Password" | env(ADMIN_PW)    |
  click button "Log in"
  expect button "Log out" is visible

  open "/products?q={prefix}"
  expect css "#bulk-delete-state[data-state='idle']" is visible

  accept dialog
  accept dialog
  click button "Delete 2 out-of-stock"
  # The form submitted, so this is a real navigation and a fresh render — the banner is the list
  # page's own, not a state the script could have written.
  expect css "p[data-state='bulk-deleted']" is visible
  expect text "Deleted 2 out-of-stock product(s)." is visible

  # And the products are actually gone, asked of the API rather than of the page that just claimed
  # it. Under the old slot both of these still returned 200 while every assertion above `click`
  # passed, which is exactly why the claim is made here.
  api GET /products/{gammaId}
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {adminToken}"
  expect status equals 404
  api GET /products/{deltaId}
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {adminToken}"
  expect status equals 404

@constructs @dialog @mixed
test "`accept dialog with` answers a confirm that has no answer to take, and says so"
  # `M159f` / `D806h` — the repository's witness for **`TF080`**, and the reason a runtime-only code
  # needs one at all. `verify-check-diagnostics.mjs` proves every diagnostic by provoking it with a
  # real `tflw check`; `TF079` and `TF080` are the two codes no `check` can emit (tflw `D801`,
  # `D802`, and `phase: 'run'` in `tflw spec --json` since `D806d`), so their proof is a passing
  # plant whose report carries the warning. The acceptance grader asserts it; nothing in this file
  # can, because a warning is not a step and does not change a verdict.
  #
  # The claim `TF080` makes is exactly what this test demonstrates: only a `prompt` has an input to
  # fill, the browser silently discards `promptText` for every other kind, and the dialog is
  # accepted **as if no `with` had been written** — so the product really is deleted, and the answer
  # really did go nowhere.
  api POST /auth/login body { email: env(ADMIN_EMAIL), password: env(ADMIN_PW) }
  expect status equals 200
  capture body.accessToken as adminToken
  api GET /categories
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {adminToken}"
  expect status equals 200
  capture body[0].id as categoryId

  let prefix = unique uuid
  api POST /products body { name: "{prefix} epsilon", price: 7.00, stock: 3, categoryId: {categoryId} }
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {adminToken}"
  expect status equals 201
  capture body.id as epsilonId

  open "/login"
  fill form
    | "Email"    | env(ADMIN_EMAIL) |
    | "Password" | env(ADMIN_PW)    |
  click button "Log in"
  expect button "Log out" is visible

  open "/products/{epsilonId}"
  accept dialog with "delete it please"
  click button "Delete product"
  # The `with` was ignored and the accept was not: the form's own onsubmit returned true, so this is
  # a real navigation to the list page.
  expect css "h1:text-is('Products')" is visible
  api GET /products/{epsilonId}
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer {adminToken}"
  expect status equals 404
